<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Awhad Ayyub</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1210</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Awhad Ayyub was an Ayyubid prince, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-adil">al-Adil</a>, who governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayyafariqin">Mayyafariqin</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Upper Mesopotamia</a> for his father from about 1200. In 1207 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahlat">Ahlat</a> from the Shah-Armens, extending Ayyubid rule to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-van">Lake Van</a>, and held it against Georgian attacks. He died in 1210, and his lands passed to his brother al-Ashraf Musa.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-awhad-ayyub.jpg" alt="Al-Awhad Ayyub" width="320" height="323" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Awhad_Najm_al-Din_Ayyub._AH_596-607_AD_1200-1210._Mayyafariqin_mint._Dated_AH_600_(AD_1194-5)_Obverse.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
